            "source_snippet": "import sys\n\ndef find_min_i(n, k, A):\n    # Initialize the running total with the initial array\n    running_total = A[:]\n    \n    # Iterate to compute the prefix sums incrementally\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        running_total[i] += running_total[i - 1]\n    \n    # Check if the initial array already contains a number >= k\n    if max(running_total) >= k:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Incrementally compute the next arrays until we find one containing a number >= k\n    i = 1\n    while True:\n        # Compute the next array by subtracting 1 from each element of the current array\n        next_array = [x - 1 for x in running_total]\n        \n        # Update the running total with the new array\n        for j in range(1, n):\n            next_array[j] += next_array[j - 1]\n        \n        # Check if the current array contains a number >= k\n        if max(next_array) >= k:\n            return i\n        \n        # Move to the next iteration\n        running_total = next_array\n        i += 1\n\n# Read input\ninput = sys.stdin.read\ndata = input().split()\n\nn = int(data[0])\nk = int(data[1])\nA = list(map(int, data[2:]))\n\n# Find and print the minimum i\nprint(find_min_i(n, k, A))",
